x86_64-linux  clang emits only the C2 and D2 variants of an *implicit* constructor and destructor on this target, and cxx1 emits C1/D1 beside them - the three names above, all of them compiler-written. A special member the compiler writes is inline by nature and has no out-of-line form to move to, which is why the written ones in this case can dodge it and these cannot. The two are identical for a class with no virtual bases and both are mergeable definitions, so the extra symbols cost object size and nothing else. implicit-move.nonames and qualified-base-call.nonames record the same fact. Darwin and Windows agree on every name.
